      "title": "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "47c9395a-8c35-55b6-a651-91416e90d84c",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/e2761021d1f97e7c18d2ceafdb5eb2d9968df932f84229d6bcf814f298a1a603.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $q=3r$\n\nII. $t=2s$\n\nIII. $p=-t$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is true. The vectors $q$ and $r$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $q$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $r$. So, $q=3r$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $t$ and $s$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $t$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $s$. So, $t\\ne 2s$.\n\n- Statement III is false. The vectors $p$ and $t$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $p$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $t$. So, $p\\ne -t$.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "5fd6807b-5c16-5fef-95f6-c848ad896609",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
