    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "16fe1696-0cce-54e1-bbd5-b42c67f4de0a",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/afa1b211a080e379fd4e5177560447b0338f5eb8fabefb91c79b9f6daf48b44f.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $m=-k$\n\nII. $a=z$\n\nIII. $k=-\\frac{1}{2}m$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $m$ and $k$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $m$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $k$. So, $m\\ne -k$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $a$ and $z$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $a$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $z$. So, $a\\ne z$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $k$ and $m$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $k$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $m$. So, $k=-\\frac{1}{2}m$.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "6885c315-9dbf-5a55-a75f-0d0d918e5ae3",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "a0cb4ed8-277f-50aa-9689-8024446c7d59",
