  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186096700,
      "id": "dc5bab6c-1ce7-58b5-a59d-cff2ce35ecca",
      "title": "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4316b41d-bc5c-57af-903f-acf90c0e38df",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/bfc874b2f6433f4dc084633c3d429bad0f9184dc52676d5a992fb0b32e7d7299.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is true. The vectors $a$ and $e$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $a$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $e$. So, $a=-e$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $e$ and $a$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $e$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $a$. So, $e\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}a$.\n\n- Statement III is false. The vectors $b$ and $d$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $b$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $d$. So, $b\\ne d$.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
