    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3a11b5af-feb0-5c33-bfb3-cdec7fd3786d",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/202c7c604d7746050b1fa583411b01571fb4d49fbd0ac06a2d4a017d5eadc50d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $g=-a$\n\nII. $z=m$\n\nIII. $a=-\\frac{1}{2}g$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $g$ and $a$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $g$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $a$. So, $g\\ne -a$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $z$ and $m$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $z$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $m$. So, $z\\ne m$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $a$ and $g$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $a$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $g$. So, $a=-\\frac{1}{2}g$.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2d55daaa-2e60-57ff-8ad2-5fe1683c0a7b",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
