  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186096700,
      "id": "dc5bab6c-1ce7-58b5-a59d-cff2ce35ecca",
      "title": "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7493fa08-df08-5ae7-948e-120204eb0e83",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/2d231c8e890eb2850883261ba645bba2d542e23cc2399866d19c4bf1032a8c3e.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $m=-z$\n\nII. $g=a$\n\nIII. $z=-\\frac{1}{2}m$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $m$ and $z$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $m$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $z$. So, $m\\ne -z$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The vectors $g$ and $a$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $g$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $a$. So, $g=a$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $z$ and $m$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $z$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $m$. So, $z=-\\frac{1}{2}m$.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ee6dd915-cb03-5a26-b739-30fb22ab48b5",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "e5a0edd1-0062-5b9f-9847-deb5e665ccab",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "III only"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "30818e2e-cb76-52e7-9e4b-b93e2271f926",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "I and III only"
            },
            {
