  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186096700,
      "id": "dc5bab6c-1ce7-58b5-a59d-cff2ce35ecca",
      "title": "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b8788484-4ab8-5009-a6f6-247535cc9050",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/a5c9da4c920b1f5b8b29f27c9b6d3ee705752499213cc0acfebb766f84095756.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $z=-m$\n\nII. $a=k$\n\nIII. $m=-\\frac{1}{2}z$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $z$ and $m$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $z$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $m$. So, $z\\ne -m$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The vectors $a$ and $k$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $a$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $k$. So, $a=k$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $m$ and $z$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $m$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $z$. So, $m=-\\frac{1}{2}z$.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "05987e57-ca37-56c9-9329-308bb7cc1e2c",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
