  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186096700,
      "id": "dc5bab6c-1ce7-58b5-a59d-cff2ce35ecca",
      "title": "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2a6df057-112c-53bf-ac46-6183b8319a14",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/031010ba45ae2461a7bc56c41c10aa21538e67682bb938aa7beef3439012e157.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $k=-z$\n\nII. $a=g$\n\nIII. $z=-\\frac{1}{2}k$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $k$ and $z$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $k$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $z$. So, $k\\ne -z$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The vectors $a$ and $g$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $a$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $g$. So, $a=g$.\n\n- Statement III is false. The vectors $z$ and $k$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $z$ is $\\frac{1}{3}$ times the magnitude of $k$. So, $z\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}k$.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "bfd057c3-56b9-50e7-ab3d-bbbcca37369a",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "4f445cb5-fbc3-515a-84ca-7d5e9c71cc58",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "I only"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "fa945957-2310-5fa9-b9d6-c5de2f41e39c",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "II only"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "4c0f1d68-31be-57dd-bc2d-5bc4a2c9ceaa",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "III only"
            },
