    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e687b692-9634-5fb7-aa3d-8a7c5d9458a5",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/b4dee3ea47962699e4433f0a405d1d2334c33aa9f419cb0c898baa2930fb0b3c.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $p=3q$\n\nII. $r=2t$\n\nIII. $s=-r$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $p$ and $q$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $p$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $q$. So, $p\\ne 3q$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The vectors $r$ and $t$ are parallel and have the same direction. The magnitude of $r$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $t$. So, $r=2t$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $s$ and $r$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $s$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $r$. So, $s=-r$.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c57bb958-293d-588f-b4bd-e27930aca1be",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
