  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186096700,
      "id": "dc5bab6c-1ce7-58b5-a59d-cff2ce35ecca",
      "title": "Identifying Parallel Vectors From a Diagram"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5a48939e-a868-5ad4-a2e3-5a3bcd10af94",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/f2a29de02610b44137729c99455a86b7695b8163847053dc4443a54a218d255e.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $t=3r$\n\nII. $q=2s$\n\nIII. $p=-q$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $t$ and $r$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $t$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $r$. So, $t\\ne 3r$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $q$ and $s$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $q$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $s$. So, $q\\ne 2s$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $p$ and $q$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $p$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $q$. So, $p=-q$.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b6279a84-40de-51a0-8076-34d31dbe3134",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
