    "question": {
      ":question/id": "727b99d7-505e-5ed0-977e-405475f2e240",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/2a571b7588d938fea4cd8d6b67512762147f715395e37ab8019dd3bd00a6cb92.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $k=-z$\n\nII. $a=g$\n\nIII. $z=-\\frac{1}{2}k$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is true. The vectors $k$ and $z$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $k$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $z$. So, $k=-z$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $a$ and $g$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $a$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $g$. So, $a\\ne g$.\n\n- Statement III is false. The vectors $z$ and $k$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $z$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $k$. So, $z\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}k$.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true.",
