    "question": {
      ":question/id": "552a6063-a5aa-5167-a132-d3ee8cf5b880",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/repairs/2026-10-19-checkbox-nonempty/patched-samples/17592186096700/assets/727e1267862b35641c46210cca21eee0e54ac75c7106463d14af8ba168e6a3bf.png)\n\nConsider the diagram that is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $g=-z$\n\nII. $m=a$\n\nIII. $z=-\\frac{1}{2}g$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "- Statement I is false. The vectors $g$ and $z$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $g$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $z$. So, $g\\ne -z$.\n\n- Statement II is false. The vectors $m$ and $a$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $m$ is $1$ times the magnitude of $a$. So, $m\\ne a$.\n\n- Statement III is true. The vectors $z$ and $g$ are parallel and have opposite directions. The magnitude of $z$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $g$. So, $z=-\\frac{1}{2}g$.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true.",
